The MAYOR’s parlour. Too much furniture. The MAYOR is arranging a plate of biscuits into a pyramid. ROSALIND, the lighthouse keeper, stands by the window in oilskins, dripping.

MAYOR
You could have changed, Rosalind.

ROSALIND
Into what? The light doesn’t wait while I find a frock.

MAYOR
It is a matter of impressions. The man from the Admiralty must see a town of standing. Of biscuits.

ROSALIND
He must see the light. Two hundred years it’s burned, and not one wreck on the Teeth in all that time.

A knock. MORDECAI enters, damp from the coach.

MORDECAI
Mordecai Finch, Admiralty. I’m told I’m expected.

MAYOR
Expected, Mr Finch? You are *anticipated*. Biscuit?

MORDECAI
Thank you, no. I’d like to see the light today, if the keeper is—

ROSALIND
The keeper is here.

MORDECAI
(turning)
Ah. Forgive me. I was told the keeper was a man called Rowe.

ROSALIND
My father. He kept it forty years. I’ve kept it six. Nobody wrote to the Admiralty about the difference, and the lamp never noticed.

MAYOR
(the pyramid collapses)
Biscuit?
